"Authorization | SCAPI | B2C Commerce API | Salesforce Developers (https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/commerce/commerce-api/guide/authorization.html)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view3\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 30\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://docs.stripe.com/api/authentication)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/markdown; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view6\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 51\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\niPayX Protocol — FINTRAC-registered FX forensic audit layer (https://www.ipayx.ai/developers)\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ipayx.ai/developers\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 25\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://university.clay.com/docs/clay-plugin\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCommas API Reference (https://apidocs.fan/)\nciteturn4view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://apidocs.fan/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAPI Accounts | BigCommerce Docs (https://developer.bigcommerce.com/docs/start/authentication)\nciteturn4view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"pattern\":\"X-Auth-Token\"}); Redirected to URL: https://docs.bigcommerce.com/developer/docs/overview/api-fundamentals/api-accounts; Total lines: 1209\nL906: #### Access tokens\nL907: \nL908: Legacy API accounts used HTTP basic authentication. They are no longer available to new stores. For more information, see cite586†API Accounts: Migrating from legacy to OAuth .\nL909: \nL910: Most of our REST endpoints and GraphQL Admin API endpoints use the `X-Auth-Token` header to authenticate to BigCommerce servers. For more about the APIs that do NOT use the `X-Auth-Token` header, consult this article’s sections on cite601†dynamic tokens and cite608†same-origin CORS authentication .\nL911: The `X-Auth-Token` header uses access tokens to authenticate requests. cite570†Create an OAuth API account to generate access tokens. Pass the access token as the value of the `X-Auth-Token` header of the request you want to authenticate.\nL912: The cite661†B2B REST Management API also uses the `X-Auth-Token` header, but requires the additional `X-Store-Hash` header to identify the store. B2B REST Management API endpoints use a different base URL (`https://api-b2b.bigcommerce.com`), so the store hash is not included in the request path as it is for other BigCommerce REST APIs. For more on B2B authentication, see cite432†Authentication for hosted storefronts .\nL913: #### The X-Auth-Client header is deprecated\nL914: \nL915: Your API account’s client ID is cite662†no longer a required header value .\nL916: \nL917: For a request to succeed, the access token’s API account must have permission to receive the response. Configure your API account with the minimum set of OAuth scopes that your implementation needs.\nL918: To find the specific OAuth scopes your requests require, consult the root API reference pages for the families of endpoints you plan to use. For example, see the cite663†OAuth scopes for the Email Templates endpoints . We also maintain a cite590†list of all our OAuth scopes .\nL919: ##### X-Auth-Token header example requests\nL920: \nL921: The following tabs contain examples of how to authenticate requests by passing an access token to the `X-Auth-Token` header.\nL922: \nL923: ###### GET request\nL924: \nL925: ###### POST request\nL926: \nL927: ###### PUT request\nL928: \nL929: ###### DELETE request\nL930: \nL931: ###### B2B REST Management API\nL932: \nL933: Example GET request with X-Auth-Token header\nL934: \nL935:     GET https://api.bigcommerce.com/stores/{{STORE_HASH}}/v... # endpoint\nL936: \nL937:     X-Auth-Token: {{access_token}}\nL938: \nL939:     Accept: application/json\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAPI Accounts | BigCommerce Docs (https://developer.bigcommerce.com/docs/start/authentication)\nciteturn4view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"pattern\":\"OAuth\"}); Redirected to URL: https://docs.bigcommerce.com/developer/docs/overview/api-fundamentals/api-accounts; Total lines: 1209\nL765: ### Migrating from legacy to OAuth\nL766: \nL767: #### Legacy API Accounts\nL768: \nL769: BigCommerce no longer issues legacy API Accounts to new stores. Existing legacy API Accounts will continue to work until further notice. Migrate to OAuth as soon as possible.\nL770: #### Benefits of migrating to OAuth\nL771: \nL772: We recommend migrating from legacy API credentials to OAuth, if you haven’t already. Migration provides a wealth of benefits, including the following:\nL773: \nL774:   * Unified requests: Send all OAuth requests to a single URL: `https://api.bigcommerce.com`. Using a common hostname prevents interruptions of service when a store’s domain or SSL/TLS certificate changes or expires.\nL775: \nL776:   * Latest and greatest APIs: BigCommerce’s V3 APIs are accessible exclusively with OAuth.\nL777:   * Webhook subscriptions: OAuth API accounts can subscribe to real-time event notifications using BigCommerce’s webhooks.\nL778: \nL779:   * Shared secrets: Use new APIs and endpoints that require shared secrets, including the cite171†Customer Login and cite172†Current Customer APIs.\nL780: \nL781:   * Zippier responses: Responses to OAuth requests use gzip compression and less of your bandwidth.\nL782:   * Better security with granular permissions: All OAuth tokens are scoped to specific operations and endpoints. If you suspect a breach, you will know which resources could be affected.\nL783: #### How to migrate\nL784: \nL785: Before you update your API connections to use OAuth instead of legacy basic authentication, take the following actions:\nL786:   * Create an API account appropriate to your use case. Keeping in mind the API endpoints your connections use, create either a store API account or an app API account per the preceding instructions. To adhere to industry-standard security practices, configure the account with the minimum OAuth scopes for your use case. If you’re using an app API account, you can always modify the scope later.\nL787:   * If you use one of our cite6†client libraries , consult the library’s documentation for establishing an optimal OAuth configuration.\nL788:   * After you create your connection, update your connection parameters as follows:\nL789:     * Use `https://api.bigcommerce.com` as the gateway URL instead of the BigCommerce store’s secure hostname. For example, route requests that formerly went to `https://store-{{store_hash}}.mybigcommerce.com/api/v2/orders/{{order_id}}` or `https://my-custom-store-domain.com/api/v2/orders/{{order_id}}` will now use `https://api.bigcommerce.com/stores/{{store_hash}}/v2/orders/{{order_id}}`.\nL790:     * Rewrite your HTTP request headers to use the `X-Auth-Token` header to pass the API account’s `access_token` instead of the `Authorization` header. For more information, see the cite596†access tokens section above.\nL791: Rate limiting works differently for OAuth API connections. For details, see the cite9†Rate Limits section of our API best practices article.\nL792: ## OAuth scopes\nL793: \nL794: Scope grants and limits a program’s ability to read and write data. Set the scopes to the minimum level of access your implementation needs.\nL795: \nL796: All OAuth scopes except `default` provide `read-only` permissions scopes so that you can limit some accounts to sending `GET` and `HEAD` requests.\nL797: \nL798: Webhooks are accessible from the default scope that is automatically accessible to all API accounts.\nL799: ### Token creation scopes\nL800: UI Name  | Permission  | Parameter  | Description  | Endpoints\nL801: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL802: Create Payments  | modify  | `store_payments_access_token_create`  | Create payment access tokens, process payments  | cite623†Payments, Access Tokens L803: cite624†Payments, Process Payment L804: Storefront API Tokens  | modify  | `store_storefront_api`  | Create GraphQL Storefront API bearer tokens  | cite625†Storefront API Token V3 L918: To find the specific OAuth scopes your requests require, consult the root API reference pages for the families of endpoints you plan to use. For example, see the cite663†OAuth scopes for the Email Templates endpoints . We also maintain a cite590†list of all our OAuth scopes .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConnect to the SP-API (https://developer-docs.amazon.com/sp-api/docs/connecting-to-the-selling-partner-api)\nciteturn4view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view5\",\"pattern\":\"refresh_token\"}); Redirected to URL: https://developer-docs.amazon/sp-api/docs/connecting-to-the-selling-partner-api; Total lines: 183\nL38: Name  | Description  | Required\nL39: --- | --- | ---\nL40: `grant_type`  | The type of access grant requested. Values:\nL41: - `refresh_token`. Use this for calling operations that require authorization from a selling partner. All operations that are not cite18†grantless operations†developer-docs.amazon.com require authorization from a selling partner. When specifying this value, include the `refresh_token` parameter.\nL43: `refresh_token`  | The LWA refresh token. Get this value when the selling partner authorizes your application. For more information, refer to cite16†Authorizing Selling Partner API applications†developer-docs.amazon.com .  | No. Include `refresh_token` for calling operations that require authorization from a selling partner. If you include `refresh_token`, do not include scope.\nL44: `scope`  | The scope of the LWA authorization grant. Values:\nL45:   * cite19†Notifications API : `sellingpartnerapi::notifications`\nL46:   * cite20†Application Management API : `sellingpartnerapi::client_credential:rotation`\nL47:   * cite21†Tracking API : `sellingpartnerapi::shipments:track`\nL48:  | No. Include scope for calling a cite18†grantless operation†developer-docs.amazon.com . If you include scope, do not include `refresh_token`.\nL49: `client_id`  | Get this value when you register your application. Refer to cite22†Viewing your developer information†developer-docs.amazon.com .  | Yes\nL50: `client_secret`  | Get this value when you register your application. Refer to cite22†Viewing your developer information†developer-docs.amazon.com .  | Yes\nL51: Example for calling an operation that requires selling partner authorization:\nL52: \nL53: [Button: HTTP]\nL54: \nL55:     `POST /auth/o2/token HTTP/l.l\nL56:     Host: api.amazon.com\nL57:     Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded;charset=UTF-8\nL58:     grant_type=refresh_token\nL59:     &refresh_token=Aztr|...\nL60:     &client_id=foodev\nL61:     &client_secret=Y76SDl2F\nL62:     `\nL63: \nL64: Example for calling a grantless operation:\nL65: [Button: HTTP]\nL66: \nL67:     `POST /auth/o2/token HTTP/l.l\nL68:     Host: api.amazon.com\nL69:     Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded;charset=UTF-8\nL70:     grant_type=client_credentials\nL71:     &scope=sellingpartnerapi::notifications\nL72:     &client_id=foodev\nL73:     &client_secret=Y76SDl2F\nL74:     `\nL75: \nL76: > ## ⭐\nL77: >\nL78: > Tip\nL79: >\nL80: > To avoid getting an untrusted certificate authority (CA) error when calling the LWA authorization server, be sure to update your trust store so that your application trusts the LWA authorization server.\nL81: A successful response includes the following values.\nL82: \nL83: Name  | Description\nL84: --- | ---\nL85: `access_token`  | The LWA access token. Maximum size: 2048 bytes.\nL86: `token_type`  | The type of token returned. Must be bearer.\nL87: `expires_in`  | The number of seconds before the LWA access token becomes invalid.\nL88: `refresh_token`  | The LWA refresh token that you submitted in the request. Maximum size: 2048 bytes.\nL89: [Button: HTTP]\nL90: \nL91:     `HTTP/l.l 200 OK\nL92:     Content-Type: application/json;charset UTF-8\nL93:     Cache-Control: no-store\nL94:     Pragma:no-cache\nL95:     {\nL96:       \"access_token\":\"Atza|IQEBLjAsAhRmHjNgHpi0U-Dme37rR6CuUpSREXAMPLE\",\nL97:       \"token_type\":\"bearer\",\nL98:       \"expires_in\":3600,\nL99:       \"refresh_token\":\"Atzr|IQEBLzAtAhRPpMJxdwVz2Nn6f2y-tpJX2DeXEXAMPLE\"\nL100:     }\nL101:     `\nL102: \nL103: For more information, visit the cite23†Authorization Code Grant†developer.amazon.com page in the Login with Amazon documentation.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAtlas Administration API Authentication Methods - Atlas - MongoDB Docs (https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/api/api-authentication/)\nciteturn4view8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view2\",\"pattern\":\"service accounts\"}); Total lines: 207\nL47: ## Important\nL48: \nL49: We recommend you use service accounts instead of API keys to authenticate to the Atlas Administration API. API keys are a legacy authentication method.\nL50: \nL51: To use the Atlas Administration API to manage your Atlas clusters, you must authenticate your API requests. The Atlas Administration API accepts the following authentication methods:\nL52: \nL53:   * Service account access tokens (cite4†OAuth 2.0)†oauth.net L54: \nL55:   * API keys (cite5†HTTP Digest Authentication)†en.wikipedia.org L56: To learn how to use service accounts and API keys to configure programmatic access to your Atlas organizations and projects, see the cite6†Atlas Administration API Getting Started guide.\nL63: ## Service Accounts Overview\nL64: \nL65: Service accounts are the recommended method to manage authentication to the Atlas Administration API. Service accounts provide improved security over API keys by using the industry standard cite4†OAuth 2.0†oauth.net protocol with the cite11†Client Credentials flow.†www.oauth.com L73:   * Generate new tokens when needed: While you can't refresh an access token, you can cite14†generate a new token when your current token is close to expiry. This prevents service interruptions and allows you to maintain authorized service account access.\nL74: \nL75:   * Revoke unnecessary tokens: When you no longer need a token, cite15†revoke the token's access to minimize security risks and ensure that only active tokens have access to your service account.\nL76: Each service account belongs to exactly one organization, and you can grant it access to any number of projects within that organization. To give an organization-level service account access to a project, see cite16†Grant Existing Service Account Access to a Project. L77: Atlas cite17†roles limit which operations a service account can authenticate with its access token. You must assign roles to service accounts as you would for users to ensure that the service account generates access tokens with the necessary permissions for the desired API calls.\nL78: ## Important\nL79: \nL80: If a project-level service account has access to the organization or to other projects, you must have the cite18†`Organization Owner` role to manage its lifecycle, including rotating or revoking its secrets.\nL81: You can't use a service account or its access token to log into Atlas through the Atlas UI. Service accounts only grant access to the Atlas Administration API, which does not include UI access or access to cluster data. To learn more about the limitations of service accounts, see cite19†MongoDB Limits and Thresholds. L82: ## API Keys Overview\nL83: ## Important\nL84: \nL85: We recommend you use service accounts instead of API keys to authenticate to the Atlas Administration API. API keys are a legacy authentication method.\nL86: \nL87: API keys are a legacy method of authenticating to the Atlas Administration API that uses cite5†HTTP Digest Authentication.†en.wikipedia.org L88: API keys have two parts: a Public Key and a Private Key. These serve the same function as a username and a password to authenticate API requests. To learn how to construct an API request using API keys, see cite13†Make an API Request. L89: Atlas hashes the Public Key and Private Key using a unique value called a nonce. The nonce is only valid for a short amount of time as per the cite5†HTTP Digest Authentication†en.wikipedia.org specification. This limited lifespan protects against replay attacks, where an attacker caches a Private Key to use it without time restriction.\nL90: Each pair of API keys belongs to only one organization, and can grant access to any number of projects in that organization. To give organization-level API keys access to a project, see cite16†Grant Existing Service Account Access to a Project. L91: \nL92: Atlas cite17†roles limit which operations API keys can perform. You must assign roles to API keys as you would for users to ensure the API keys have the necessary permissions for the desired API calls.\nL93: You can't use API keys to log into Atlas through the Atlas UI. API keys only grant access to the Atlas Administration API, which does not include UI access or access to cluster data.\nL94: ## Next Steps\nL95: \nL96: To learn how to use and manage service accounts and API keys, see the following procedures:\nL97: \nL98:   * cite20†Grant Programmatic Access to an Organization L99: \nL100:   * cite21†Grant Programmatic Access to a Project L101: \nL102:   * cite22†Manage Programmatic Access to an Organization L103: \nL104:   * cite23†Manage Programmatic Access to a Project L105: \nL106:   * cite24†Manage Programmatic Access to Multiple Organizations L107: \nL108:   * cite14†Generate Service Account Token L109: \nL110:   * cite15†Revoke Service Account Token L111: \nL112: cite25†Back Admin API Overview L113: cite26†Next Rotate Service Account Secrets L114: \nL115: Rate this page\nL116: \nL117: On this page\nL118: \nL119:   * cite27†Service Accounts Overview L120:   * cite28†API Keys Overview L121:   * cite29†Next Steps L122: \nL123: On this page\nL124: \nL125:   * cite27†Service Accounts Overview L126:   * cite28†API Keys Overview L127:   * cite29†Next Steps L128: \nL129: Documentation\nL130: \nL131: Database\nL132: \nL133: VoyageAI Models\nL134: \nL135:   *   *   *   *   * MongoDB Atlas\nL136:     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *   * Enterprise Management Tools\nL137: \nL138:     *     *     *   * Self-Managed Deployments\nL139: \nL140: Select a version\nL141: \nL142:     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *   * Infrastructure As Code\nL143: \nL144:     *     *     *     *     *     *     * * * *\nL145: \nL146:   *   *   * Documentation\nL147: \nL148: Database\nL149: \nL150: VoyageAI Models\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nlinear.app (https://linear.app/developers/graphql)\nciteturn4view9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view4\",\"pattern\":\"personal API\"}); Total lines: 468\nL66: ## Authenticationcite38†⁠ L67: \nL68: The Linear API supports personal API keys and OAuth2 authentication.\nL69: ### OAuthcite39†⁠ L70: \nL71: If you’re building an application for others to use, we recommend you use cite12†OAuth2 authentication . Once you complete the authentication flow and acquire an access token, pass it with the header `Authorization: Bearer <ACCESS_TOKEN>`\nL72: \nL73:     `curl \\\nL74:       -X POST \\\nL75:       -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\nL76:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer <ACCESS_TOKEN>\" \\\nL77:       --data '{ \"query\": \"{ issues { nodes { id title } } }\" }' \\\nL78:       https://api.linear.app/graphql`\nL79: ### Personal API Keyscite40†⁠ L80: \nL81: For personal scripts API keys are the easiest way to access the API. Visit cite41†Security & access settings to create and manage them.\nL82: \nL83: To authenticate your requests, you need to pass the API key with header: `Authorization: <API_KEY>`\nL84: \nL85:     `curl \\\nL86:       -X POST \\\nL87:       -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\nL88:       -H \"Authorization: <Replace this with your API Key>\" \\\nL89:       --data '{ \"query\": \"{ issues { nodes { id title } } }\" }' \\\nL90:       https://api.linear.app/graphql`\nL304:   * cite37†Endpoint L305:   * cite38†Authentication L306:   * cite39†OAuth L307:   * cite40†Personal API Keys L308:   * cite42†Error handling L309:   * cite43†Linear SDK L310:   * cite44†Getting Started L311:   * cite46†Queries & Mutations L312:   * cite47†Creating & Editing Issues L313:   * cite48†Accessing Images L314:   * cite49†Adding mentions in Markdown L315:   * cite50†Adding collapsible sections in Markdown L316:   * cite51†Fetching Updates L317:   * cite53†Other Examples L318:   * cite54†Queries L319:   * cite55†Archived resources L320:   * cite56†Support L321: \nL322:     `https://api.linear.app/graphql`\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAuthentication (https://developer.monday.com/api-reference/docs/authentication)\nciteturn4view10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view5\",\"pattern\":\"personal API\"}); Total lines: 117\nL23: #\nL24: \nL25: Token permissions\nL26: \nL27: Personal tokens mirror all permission levels set in the monday.com UI, including cite11†board†support.monday.com , cite12†column†support.monday.com , cite13†item†support.monday.com , or cite14†account†support.monday.com access.\nL28: \nL29: > For example: If you don't have permission to access a certain workspace via the UI, you won't have permission using your personal API token either.\nL31: #\nL32: \nL33: Accessing your token\nL34: \nL35: You can access your API token in two ways, depending on your cite16†user type†support.monday.com .\nL36: \nL37: ##\nL38: \nL39: In the Developer Center (all users)\nL40: \nL41: All cite17†users with API access can follow these steps to access their API token:\nL42: \nL43:   1. In your monday.com account, click on your profile picture in the top right corner.\nL44:   2. Select Developers. This will open the Developer Center in another tab.\nL45:   3. Click API token > Show.\nL46:   4. Copy your personal token.\nL47: \nL48: cite18†Image†files.readme.io L49: ##\nL50: \nL51: In the Administration tab (account admins only)\nL52: \nL53: Account admins can use the Developer Center steps above or access their token via the Administration tab:\nL54: \nL55:   1. In your monday.com account, click on your profile picture in the top right corner.\nL56:   2. Select Administration > Connections > Personal API token.\nL57:   3. Copy your personal token.\nL58: \nL59: cite19†Image†files.readme.io L60: #\nL61: \nL62: Making requests with your token\nL63: \nL64: Once you have your token, you can make requests with the API by passing the token in the `Authorization` header.\nL65: \nL66: [Button: cURL]\nL67: \nL68:     `curl -X POST https://api.monday.com/v2 \\\nL69:       -H \"Authorization: xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\" \\\nL70:       -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\nL71:       -d '{\"query\": \"query { me { id name } }\"}'`\nL72: #\nL73: \nL74: Regenerating a token\nL75: \nL76: API tokens can be regenerated at any time. However, this will immediately invalidate your current token, so be sure to update any integrations using it.\nL77: \nL78: ##\nL79: \nL80: How to regenerate a token\nL81: \nL82: ###\nL83: \nL84: In the Developer Center\nL85: \nL86:   1. In your monday.com account, click on your profile picture in the top right corner.\nL87:   2. Select Developers. This will open the Developer Center in another tab.\nL88:   3. Click API token > Regenerate.\nL89: ###\nL90: \nL91: In the Administration tab\nL92: \nL93:   1. In your monday.com account, click on your profile picture in the top right corner.\nL94:   2. Select Administration > Connections > Personal API token.\nL95:   3. Click Regenerate.\nL96: \nL97: Updated 11 days ago\nL98: \nL99: * * *\nL100: \nL101: cite20†The basics cite21†Versioning L102: \nL103: Did this page help you?\nL104: \nL105: Yes\nL106: \nL107: No\nL108: \nL109: Copy Page\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nYouTube Transcript API Reference | TranscriptAPI.com (https://transcriptapi.com/docs/api/)\nciteturn4view11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view4\",\"pattern\":\"Bearer\"}); Total lines: 2090\nL811: Terminal window\nL812: \nL813:     `\nL814: \nL815:     # First page\nL816: \nL817:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/channel/search?channel=@TED&q=innovation\" \\\nL818: \nL819:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL820: \nL821: \nL822: \nL823: \nL824:     # Next page\nL825: \nL826:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/channel/search?continuation=4qmFsgKlARIYVVV1QVhGa2dz...\" \\\nL827: \nL828:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL829: \nL830:     `\nL911: Terminal window\nL912: \nL913:     `\nL914: \nL915:     # First page (using @handle)\nL916: \nL917:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/channel/videos?channel=@TED\" \\\nL918: \nL919:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL920: \nL921: \nL922: \nL923: \nL924:     # First page (using channel URL)\nL925: \nL926:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/channel/videos?channel=https://www.youtube.com/@TED\" \\\nL927: \nL928:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL929: \nL930: \nL931: \nL932: \nL933:     # Next page\nL934:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/channel/videos?continuation=4qmFsgKlARIYVVV1QVhGa2dz...\" \\\nL935: \nL936:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL937: \nL938:     `\nL1011: Example:\nL1012: \nL1013: Terminal window\nL1014: \nL1015:     `\nL1016: \nL1017:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/channel/latest?channel=@TED\" \\\nL1018: \nL1019:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL1020: \nL1021:     `\nL1022: \nL1023: * * *\nL1101: Terminal window\nL1102: \nL1103:     `\nL1104: \nL1105:     # First page (using playlist ID)\nL1106: \nL1107:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/playlist/videos?playlist=PLrAXtmErZgOeiKm4sgNOknGvNjby9efdf\" \\\nL1108: \nL1109:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL1110: \nL1111: \nL1112: \nL1113: \nL1114:     # First page (using playlist URL)\nL1115: \nL1116:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/playlist/videos?playlist=https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLrAXtmErZgOeiKm4sgNOknGvNjby9efdf\" \\\nL1117:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL1118: \nL1119: \nL1120: \nL1121: \nL1122:     # Next page\nL1123: \nL1124:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/playlist/videos?continuation=4qmFsgKlARIYVVV1QVhGa2dz...\" \\\nL1125: \nL1126:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL1127: \nL1128:     `\nL1266: ### 401 Unauthorized\nL1267: \nL1268: cite39†Section titled “401 Unauthorized” L1269: \nL1270: Includes a `WWW-Authenticate` header:\nL1271: \nL1272:     `\nL1273: \nL1274:     WWW-Authenticate: Bearer\nL1275: \nL1276:     `\nL1335: Terminal window\nL1336: \nL1337:     `\nL1338: \nL1339:     # Basic request\nL1340: \nL1341:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/transcript?video_url=dQw4w9WgXcQ\" \\\nL1342: \nL1343:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL1344: \nL1345: \nL1346: \nL1347: \nL1348:     # With all parameters\nL1349: \nL1350:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/transcript?video_url=dQw4w9WgXcQ&format=json&include_timestamp=true&send_metadata=true\" \\\nL1351: \nL1352:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL1353: \nL1354: \nL1355: \nL1356: \nL1357:     # Text format without timestamps\nL1358: \nL1359:     curl -X GET \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2/youtube/transcript?video_url=dQw4w9WgXcQ&format=text&include_timestamp=false\" \\\nL1360: \nL1361:       -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL1362: \nL1363:     `\nL1364: \nL1365:     `\nL1366: \nL1367:     import requests\nL1368: \nL1369:     import json\nL1370: \nL1371: \nL1372: \nL1373: \nL1374:     # Configuration\nL1375: \nL1376:     API_KEY = \"YOUR_API_KEY\"\nL1377: \nL1378:     BASE_URL = \"https://transcriptapi.com/api/v2\"\nL1379: \nL1380: \nL1381: \nL1382: \nL1383:     def get_transcript(video_url, format=\"json\", include_timestamp=True, send_metadata=False):\nL1384:         \"\"\"Fetch YouTube video transcript\"\"\"\nL1385: \nL1386: \nL1387: \nL1388: \nL1389:         headers = {\nL1390: \nL1391:             \"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {API_KEY}\"\nL1392: \nL1393:         }\nL1394: \nL1395: \nL1396: \nL1397: \nL1398:         params = {\nL1399: \nL1400:             \"video_url\": video_url,\nL1401: \nL1402:             \"format\": format,\nL1403: \nL1404:             \"include_timestamp\": include_timestamp,\nL1405: \nL1406:             \"send_metadata\": send_metadata\nL1407: \nL1408:         }\nL1409: \nL1410: \nL1411: \nL1412: \nL1413:         try:\nL1414: \nL1415:             response = requests.get(\n"
